Step 1 — POS Layer 1: Qualitative Log-Odds Adjusters

Status: Implemented.

What it does:

Takes an industry phase/therapeutic-area base rate and shifts it up or down using asset-specific evidence. The model works
in log-odds space rather than adding directly to probability.

Core formula:

```text
base_log_odds = log(base_probability / (1 - base_probability))

adjusted_log_odds =
    base_log_odds
  + endpoint_adjustment
  + moa_precedent_adjustment
  + sample_size_adjustment
  + safety_adjustment
  + competitive_pressure_adjustment
  + biomarker_selection_adjustment
  + prior_phase_data_adjustment
  + regulatory_designation_adjustment
  + modality_overlay_adjustment

adjusted_POS = 1 / (1 + exp(-adjusted_log_odds))
```

Why log-odds:

If probabilities are adjusted directly, they can exceed 100% or behave inconsistently near extremes. Log-odds keeps all
final probabilities bounded between 0 and 1 and makes evidence increments additive.

Main POS inputs:

| Input | What it measures | Current scoring approach | Key weakness |
|---|---|---|---|
| Therapeutic area / phase base rate | Industry starting point | Config-driven base rates | TA averages may not match exact indication/modality |
| Endpoint type | Clinical/regulatory endpoint strength | TA-specific log-odds maps | Endpoint nuance still needs analyst review |
| MoA precedent | Whether mechanism has human/approval precedent | Log-odds adjustment plus rescue signals | Novel biology can be mis-scored without expert review |
| Sample size adequacy | Whether trial can support inference | Scored adjustment | Does not replace statistical review |
| Safety | Cleanliness of adverse-event profile | Penalizes safety concerns; cap logic exists | Safety severity often requires manual judgment |
| Competitive pressure | Regulatory/commercial bar | Adjustment for crowding/bar | May duplicate competition in revenue if not reviewed |
| Biomarker selection | Enrichment and biological validation | Biomarker strength adjustment | Biomarkers can be over-weighted if not clinically validated |
| Prior phase data | Human evidence quality | Prior data strength adjustment | Cross-trial comparability is hard |
| Breakthrough / designation | FDA process signal | Small adjustment, not large approval boost | Designation is not efficacy proof |
| Gene/cell therapy overlay | Durability, redosing, manufacturing, safety | Additive overlay | CMC and durability risk can be asset-specific |

Guardrails:

| Guardrail | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Positive/negative caps | Prevent stacking many signals into unrealistic POS |
| BTD small boost | Avoid treating regulatory designation as approval probability |
| Accelerated approval NDA discount | Accounts for confirmatory/conversion risk |
| Modality overlays | Penalize gene/cell-specific safety, durability, CMC issues |

What the POS layer should not be used for:

| Misuse | Why it is wrong |
|---|---|
| Treating POS as a true calibrated probability for every indication | Calibration is still limited |
| Treating biomarker correction as clinical benefit | Biomarker evidence can be weak without outcome validation |
| Treating a single strong signal as approval probability | Approval requires remaining phases and regulatory success |

